Native DXSpider user registration system with historical request storage,
password management, and optional non-blocking email and Telegram
notifications.

1. Installation
---------------

For email and/or Telegram notifications, the template files must be
present in:

  /spider/local_data/reg_templates/


2. Configuration
----------------

Add the following settings to /spider/local/DXVars.pm:

# Enabling registration forces:
#   $reqreg    = 1;
#   $passwdreq = 0;
#
# When disabled, DXReg does not alter the traditional DXSpider
# reqreg/passwdreq configuration.
$reg_enable = 1;

# Length used only for automatically generated passwords.
# It does not impose a length policy on passwords chosen by users.
$reg_password_length = 10;

# Default template directory
$reg_template_dir = "$local_data/reg_templates";


Email notifications are optional. To enable them, also configure:

$reg_email_enable = 1;    # 0 = disabled

$reg_email_smtp   = 'smtp.example.net';
$reg_email_port   = 465;
$reg_email_user   = 'sysop@example.net';
$reg_email_pass   = '...';
$reg_email_from   = 'sysop@example.net';
$reg_email_admin  = 'sysop@example.net';


Administrative Telegram notifications are also optional.
To enable them:

$reg_telegram_enable = 1;    # 0 = disabled

$reg_telegram_token  = '...';
$reg_telegram_chatid = '...';


The complete configuration example is supplied in:

  /spider/perl/DXVars.pm.issue

registration.json is created automatically on first use at:

  /spider/local_data/registration.json

DXReg does NOT generate or overwrite the template directory or its files.
They are supplied with this package so the SYSOP can customize them
without DXReg replacing local changes.

When $reg_enable = 0, DXReg does not modify the normal DXSpider
$reqreg / $passwdreq configuration.

3. Commands
-----------

Command names:

  request/register
  show/register
  accept/register
  reject/register
  remove/register

The separate show/registered command continues to display registered
DXUser accounts; it does NOT show DXReg requests.

Only for the show command family, the second word must be written in full
as register or registered.
sh/register and sh/registered remain valid, but show/reg, sh/reg and
show/registere are rejected. All other DXSpider command abbreviations
retain their original behavior.

DXWeb and DXWeb Admin registration actions use the Web.pm reg_* protocol
and call DXReg APIs directly, not the five console command paths.
The protocol message names have not changed.
This is a source-code compatibility finding, not a substitute for an
end-to-end test on a running node.


3.1 request/register
--------------------

Normal user:

  request/register <email> <language> [ssid-list]

The callsign (CALL) is always taken from the connected session.

SYSOP:

  request/register <call> <email> <language> [ssid-list]


Examples:

  request/register user@example.net ES
  request/register user@example.net EN 1,2,5
  request/register EA3XYZ user@example.net ES 1-5

Only one PENDING request per CALL is allowed.
Previous ACCEPTED, REJECTED or REMOVED records remain in the history.

Valid SSIDs are 1..99. SSID 0 and zero-padded forms such as 01..09
are rejected.

The language is stored as an uppercase two-letter code.
The web registration form offers a language selector and defaults to EN.
Notification template lookup first tries the selected language;
if the corresponding template is not installed, DXReg automatically
uses the equivalent English (.EN) template.

3.2 show/register
-----------------

  show/register
  show/register <call>
  show/register <request-id>

Without arguments, lists PENDING requests.

With a CALL, displays the complete registration history for that callsign.
With an ID, displays that request in the same readable history format.

3.3 accept/register
-------------------

  accept/register <request-id|call> [note]

The CALL form resolves the unique PENDING request for that callsign.

Acceptance:

- preserves already registered SSIDs;
- adds newly requested SSIDs;
- never interprets omitted SSIDs as removal;
- reuses the base callsign password if one exists;
- otherwise generates a new password;
- synchronizes the same password across the base callsign and all
  accepted/current SSIDs;
- stores requested_ssids and accepted_ssids separately in history;
- never stores the password in registration.json or debug logs.

3.4 reject/register
-------------------

  reject/register <request-id|call> [note]

Marks the PENDING request REJECTED and optionally stores an
administrative note.
A later request for the same CALL creates a new historical record.

3.5 remove/register
-------------------

  remove/register <call> [note]

Acts on the base callsign and every existing CALL-1 .. CALL-99
DXUser record:

- preserves the DXUser records;
- sets registered=0;
- removes the password;
- creates a historical REMOVED record with the affected SSIDs;
- disconnects affected connected sessions after persisting the changes,
  so that the next login loads the new state.

A disconnection failure is logged but does not roll back a registration
removal that has already been persisted.

4. Persistence
--------------

The registration request workflow history is stored in:

  /spider/local_data/registration.json

The effective registration and password state remains in DXUser.

The JSON file stores administrative history only, including:

- ID
- CALL
- email
- language
- requested SSIDs
- accepted/affected SSIDs
- status
- source
- IP
- timestamps
- processed_by
- optional note

Passwords are never stored in the JSON file.

5. Notifications
----------------

Notifications are optional.

New registration request:

- optional email to the SYSOP
- optional Telegram message to the SYSOP

Acceptance/rejection:

- optional email to the user

Email and Telegram do not block the DXSpider main loop:

- SMTP runs in Mojo::IOLoop::Subprocess
- Telegram uses Mojo::UserAgent asynchronously

Logs record notification states such as:

  queued
  sent
  failed

without logging passwords, SMTP credentials or Telegram tokens.

Templates are UTF-8. Email bodies use MIME UTF-8/Base64 and
Telegram supports HTML formatting in its templates.

6. Dependencies
---------------

  - Net::SMTP
  - Net::SMTP::SSL
  - Authen::SASL
  - IO::Socket::SSL
  - Mojo::UserAgent
  - standard CA certificates

Telegram does not require curl.

7. Notes
--------

show/register, accept/register, reject/register and remove/register are
SYSOP operations.
request/register is available to both normal users and SYSOPs.

Registration operations are local to the node where they are executed.
In V2, there is no automatic propagation between nodes.

7.1 Web integration extension (DXReg 1.2, 24-Sep-2026)

The registration mechanism is fully integrated into DXSpider Web Admin.
The SYSOP can manage the registration workflow directly from the web
interface, including viewing and searching registration requests and
their history, as well as accepting, rejecting or removing registrations.

Web registration requests may also store an optional name (maximum 80
characters) and a requester comment (maximum 500 characters).
The existing note field remains the SYSOP's note when processing
the request.

list_history() and search_history() provide native read APIs for
administration. CALL and CALL-SSID searches are grouped by base callsign
and sorted newest first. Web code must use these APIs and must not
access registration.json directly.

EOF
